  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">ENV - Combating Wildlife Crime in Kenya Program</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Kenya):  Using FY 2021 funding, USAID will continue to support the Kenya Wildlife Service (KWS) and the Ministry of Tourism and Wildlife to scale up their ability to combat wildlife trafficking activities. The award seeks to: i) improve  organizational capacity and support policy reforms; ii) maintain connectivity and improve ecosystem integrity and resilience; iii)  enhance partnership, community engagement and improve Wildlife Protection &amp; Security; and vi) secure conservation financing by diversification of wildlife and tourism enterprises. The project supports Kenya&apos;s sustainability efforts by aligning and informing relevant deliverables under the new National Wildlife Conservation and Management Strategy which KWS is responsible for implementing. The KWS five-year Strategic Plan (2019- 2024) includes: the Implementation Letter -IL No. ENV-MoTW-1with the Ministry of Tourism and Wildlife for Projects and Activities under USAID Grant Agreement No. 615-DOAG-KEN-20-00, priority listing the 2018 KWS Organizational Risk Assessment Report.The program also supports several U.S. government priorities, including the USAID Biodiversity Policy, the U.S. National Strategy for Combating Wildlife Trafficking, the Eliminate, Neutralize and Disrupt (END) Wildlife Trafficking Act and USAID&apos;s Gender Equality and Women Empowerment (GEWE) Policy.   The conservation and management of  wildlife and landscapes is a key element of nature based solutions for climate mitigation. Through the implementation of this activity, it will advance the U.S. Foreign Policy and National Security on tackling the climate crisis. $800,000 and $100,000 of funding within this IM contributes to meeting the required &quot;combating wildlife trafficking&quot; of $3,800,000 and adaptation indirect of $1,300,000 earmarks.</narrative>
  </description>
